    <node id="Paper_205">
      <data key="d0">Paper</data>
      <data key="d1">VAST</data>
      <data key="d2">2019</data>
      <data key="d3">FAIRVIS: Visual Analytics for Discovering Intersectional Bias in Machine Learning</data>
      <data key="d4">10.1109/vast47406.2019.8986948</data>
      <data key="d5">http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/VAST47406.2019.8986948</data>
      <data key="d6">46.0</data>
      <data key="d7">56.0</data>
      <data key="d8">C</data>
      <data key="d9">The growing capability and accessibility of machine learning has led to its application to many real-world domains and data about people. Despite the benefits algorithmic systems may bring, models can reflect, inject, or exacerbate implicit and explicit societal biases into their outputs, disadvantaging certain demographic subgroups. Discovering which biases a machine learning model has introduced is a great challenge, due to the numerous definitions of fairness and the large number of potentially impacted subgroups. We present FAIRVIS, a mixed-initiative visual analytics system that integrates a novel subgroup discovery technique for users to audit the fairness of machine learning models. Through FAIRVIS, users can apply domain knowledge to generate and investigate known subgroups, and explore suggested and similar subgroups. FAIRVIS's coordinated views enable users to explore a high-level overview of subgroup performance and subsequently drill down into detailed investigation of specific subgroups. We show how FAIRVIS helps to discover biases in two real datasets used in predicting income and recidivism. As a visual analytics system devoted to discovering bias in machine learning, FAIRVIS demonstrates how interactive visualization may help data scientists and the general public understand and create more equitable algorithmic systems.</data>
      <data key="d10">Ángel Alexander Cabrera;Will Epperson;Fred Hohman;Minsuk Kahng;Jamie Morgenstern;Duen Horng Chau</data>
      <data key="d11">Ángel Alexander Cabrera;Will Epperson;Fred Hohman;Minsuk Kahng;Jamie Morgenstern;Duen Horng Chau</data>
      <data key="d12">Georgia Institute of Technology;Georgia Institute of Technology;Georgia Institute of Technology;Georgia Institute of Technology;Georgia Institute of Technology;Georgia Institute of Technology</data>
      <data key="d13">10.1109/tvcg.2017.2744718;10.1109/vast.2017.8585720;10.1109/tvcg.2016.2598828;10.1109/tvcg.2018.2865044;10.1109/tvcg.2017.2744718</data>
      <data key="d14">Machine learning fairness,visual analytics,intersectional bias,subgroup discovery</data>
      <data key="d15">107.0</data>
      <data key="d16">106.0</data>
      <data key="d17">38.0</data>
      <data key="d18">2108.0</data>
      <data key="d19" />
      <data key="d20" />
    </node>
    <node id="Author_Ángel Alexander Cabrera">
      <data key="d0">Author</data>
      <data key="d21">Ángel Alexander Cabrera</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Author_Will Epperson">
      <data key="d0">Author</data>
      <data key="d21">Will Epperson</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Author_Jamie Morgenstern">
      <data key="d0">Author</data>
      <data key="d21">Jamie Morgenstern</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Author_Duen Horng Chau">
      <data key="d0">Author</data>
      <data key="d21">Duen Horng Chau</data>
